    "doc_text": "AFP_ENG_19970425.0290\n\nTIRANA, April 25 , 1997 (AFP)\n\nAlbania's central bank gets new governor\n\n\n\n\nAlbanian President Sali Berisha on Friday appointed a new head for the country's central bank, which has been without a governor for two months, a presidential spokesman told AFP.\n\nQamil Tusha, 40, will replace Kristaq Luniku, who left Albania two months ago for the United States, a Tusha aide said.\n\nTusha was previously deputy director of the Albanian Trade Bank, a state institution. According to opposition Socialist newspapers, Tusha is a close associate of Berisha.\n\nLuniku, 35, had warned of the dangers of pyramid schemes -- the bogus savings schemes whose collapse sparked widespread unrest in January that developed into a full-scale insurgency.\n\nAccording to the Albanian press, he went to the United States to take up a post as adviser to the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank.",
